<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brocken</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brocken is the highest mountain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, rising to 1,141 metres. Wrapped in fog for much of the year, it is famed in German folklore as the gathering place of witches on Walpurgis Night, a scene used by Goethe in Faust. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it lay in the East German border zone and housed Soviet and East German listening posts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brocken.jpg" alt="Brocken" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axel Hindemith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brocken_vom_Torfhaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Brocken, also called the Blocksberg, stands near Schierke between the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and is the highest peak in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the easternmost mountain there. It consists mainly of granite, which emerged about 300 million years ago towards the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> mountain-building phase. Its summit lies above the natural tree line, and the mountain has a microclimate resembling that of mountains nearly 1,000 metres higher. Mists and fogs shroud it up to 300 days a year, and its mean annual temperature is only 2.9 °C. The Brocken spectre, a climber's shadow cast upon fog, is a common phenomenon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first documented ascent was made in 1572 by the physician and botanist Johannes Thal. In 1736 Count Christian Ernst of Stolberg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> had the Wolkenhäuschen, a small refuge still preserved, built on the summit, and the first inn followed around 1800. Between 1821 and 1825 Carl Friedrich Gauss used the line of sight to the Großer Inselsberg for triangulation in the geodesic survey of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><p>The Brocken Garden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s first alpine garden, was laid out in 1890, and the narrow-gauge Brocken Railway opened on 27 March 1899. In 1935 the Deutsche Reichspost made the first television broadcast from the mountain, and in the following year the first television tower in the world was built there, carrying the first live television broadcast of the Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p>On 17 April 1945 American bombing destroyed the Brocken Hotel and the weather station. American troops held the mountain until April 1947, when it passed to the Soviet occupation zone. From August 1961 it lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>'s border zone and became a military exclusion zone, with extensive installations on the summit. Two large listening stations, one of the Soviet GRU and one of the East German Ministry for State Security, could capture radio traffic across almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. The mountain was opened to the public again on 3 December 1989 during a demonstration walk, and the last Russian soldier left on 30 March 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Brocken lies within Harz National Park, declared in 1990, and its summit is a popular destination for visitors. Steam trains of the Brocken Railway climb to a station at 1,125 metres, and the Brockenhaus at the summit holds a museum on the mountain's history. Folklore has long linked the mountain with witches and devils, and Goethe took up the legends in his tragedy Faust. The Goethe Way, a well-known trail to the summit, is named after him because he more or less followed the route in 1777.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brocken” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Repeated divisions of the family lands produced a separate line of Stolberg-Wernigerode in 1645, ruling the town and the northern Harz around the <b>Brocken</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-stolberg-wernigerode">County of Stolberg-Wernigerode</a></li></ul></section>
